I’ll inspect the measurement, chemical context, and the frozen PXRD workflow first, then build a candidate inventory and refine it in GSAS‑II. I’ll keep phase inclusion residual-led and verify the exported CIFs, fractions, profile, and project against one another.

The scan is a high-background Cu Kα pattern from the BaO₂–WO₃ system at 200 °C, so the first inventory must include unreacted precursors as seriously as Ba–W–O products. I’m now extracting peak positions and querying the frozen COD index for exact-composition records and physically related polymorphs.

The peak inventory is already coherent: monoclinic γ‑WO₃ explains the characteristic 23–25° triplet and its split higher-angle families, while tetragonal BaO₂ explains the 26–27°, 43°, 47°, and broad high-angle families. I’m fitting that two-phase baseline first; BaWO₄ remains on the watch list because its strongest lines overlap, but its unique 31.9° family is not visibly present.

The two-phase project is refining now. The first passes keep texture, roughness, size, and strain neutral while solving only the smooth background, scales, specimen shift, cells, and a shared nonnegative U/W/X profile; this makes the later phase-specific broadening tests interpretable.

The first simultaneous size/strain trial was deliberately rejected: GSAS drove both phase widths to opposing physical limits and reported singular correlations. I’m now comparing size and strain one at a time, phase by phase, under a fixed common profile—the reversible check needed to distinguish real broadening from parameter tradeoff.

The bounded physical model now gives a stable two-phase fit with plausible common Uiso values and crystallite sizes (~0.11 µm WO₃, ~0.08 µm BaO₂), but isotropic strain sits at the laboratory’s 8000 ppm ceiling. The remaining residual is family-dependent, so I’m testing only the three generator-supported uniaxial strain axes for each phase while holding the accepted size/intensity terms fixed.

The two-phase inventory is now stable: monoclinic γ-WO₃ plus tetragonal BaO₂. I’m finishing the line-broadening comparisons, then I’ll challenge trace BaWO₄ and texture before freezing the final replayable GSAS-II model.

The combined broadening trials reject BaO₂ anisotropy: the apparent gains either hit limits or break tetragonal equivalence. I’m retaining only the reproducible WO₃ [001] broadening candidate and am now testing whether an interior common profile can reproduce it without boundary-driven parameters.

The interior-profile grid is still computing; each branch is independently reconverging background, scales, shift, cells, and both phase sizes. The purpose is to separate real sample broadening from an instrument solution parked at its allowed limits.

The profile grid found a clean interior solution (U/W/X = 35/4/5.5) with essentially unchanged fit and no frozen variables. A strain grid independently confirms that both phases are genuinely very broad/high-strain; reducing strain degrades the whole-angle fit monotonically rather than being absorbed by crystallite size.

The BaWO₄ watch-list challenge converged reproducibly to only ~0.5 wt% and improved Rwp by 0.014 percentage point. That is below what scale alone can establish, so I’m checking its scale significance and its unique 17.3°/31.9° families before deciding whether it is a real trace phase or an overlap/noise surrogate.

The trace-phase test is chemically plausible but fails the residual-led criterion: BaWO₄’s unique 31.85° peak is absent and explicitly worsens when the phase is added, while its nominal scale is borrowed from overlaps. I’m excluding it unless the remaining texture trials reveal a coherent independent signal.

Both accepted texture terms are replay-stable and symmetry-consistent: WO₃ favors [111] (MD ≈ 0.917) and BaO₂ favors the tetragonally equivalent [100]/[010] family (MD ≈ 1.096). Their combined improvement is additive, unlike the rejected trace phase. I’m now testing only the small structural-coordinate adjustments allowed by the source-model uncertainty.

The unconstrained WO₃ coordinate fit lowers Rwp strongly but drives oxygen sites by up to ~1 Å, far beyond the generator’s ≤0.05 Å oxygen RMS and into chemically dubious geometry, so it is rejected as intensity overfit. I’m separating heavy-atom and individual oxygen contributions; only small, source-consistent shifts will be retained.

No atomic coordinate met the physical mismatch envelope independently: the least-squares gains require 0.08–0.97 Å shifts versus the allowed ≤0.04 Å W and ≤0.05 Å O RMS. I’m therefore retaining the chemically screened source coordinates, while keeping the well-determined refined cells, Uiso values, texture, sizes, and scales. Next is the required full-range reopen/replay and final trace-phase re-challenge.

The reopened full-range model is numerically stable (Rwp changes <0.00004 percentage point) and retains 69.0/31.0 wt% across replays. The final BaWO₄ challenge again borrows ~0.5 wt% from overlaps but contradicts its strongest unique 31.85° line, so the submitted crystalline inventory remains two phases.

The final GPX has now passed a read-only atomic replay over all 8,301 original points with no profile-review flags, and both exported CIFs match the replay exports byte-for-byte. I’m generating the exact replay profile and independent full-range statistics now, then I’ll write the concise evidence/uncertainty report.

The full-profile plot confirms that peak positions and widths are broadly adequate, while the largest residuals are intensity—not inventory—errors. I’m making one final, physically constrained coordinate test using the source WO₆ bond network; this can recover small source-model mismatch without allowing the ~1 Å oxygen drift seen in the rejected free-coordinate fit.

The bond-network restraint behaves as intended but is still too weak at ordinary weights: it cuts the intensity residual yet permits oxygen RMS ≈0.55 Å. Stronger branches are running now; acceptance requires oxygen RMS ≤0.05 Å and no degraded source geometry, regardless of Rwp.

The direct coordinate-envelope test is computationally heavier because each branch regenerates the full reflection Jacobian. It is enforcing the actual source-mismatch scale (0.02–0.07 Å) as hard absolute coordinate bounds; this is the decisive check on whether any intensity gain survives a physically permitted structure.

A physically projected coordinate model does survive: scaling the data-supported displacement direction to the generator’s exact element-wise limits gives WO₃ RMS shifts of 0.050 Å (O) and 0.030 Å (W), no frozen terms, and Rwp 8.322%. I’m screening that geometry and testing the single symmetry-allowed BaO₂ peroxide coordinate on the same bounded basis.

The bounded coordinate model improves every 10° interval above 20° and passes the same source-level geometry screen; it also makes the BaO₂ O–O distance more chemically plausible (1.415 Å). I’m adopting it with an explicit report caveat that these small coordinates are prior-limited, while phase identity, cells, and fractions are much more robust.

All final checks pass: `result.json` parses as two valid quantified models, `fit.csv` matches all 8,301 observed points and is recognized by the scorer, the replayed phase fractions agree exactly, and both CIFs agree with the final GPX exports.

Completed. The crystalline model is:

- 68.78 wt% monoclinic γ-WO₃, P2₁/n
- 31.22 wt% tetragonal BaO₂, I4/mmm

Fractions are normalized over modeled crystalline phases. Full-range replay gives Rwp 8.263%, Rp 6.071%, and GOF 2.273. Sub-percent BaWO₄ cannot be excluded reliably but was not supported by its strongest unique reflection.

